---
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_TAGLINE } from '../config/site'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

/**
 * The about page: what the site teaches, how it works, what it commits not
 * to do. Finished prose, not a draft stub — see the voice guide for the
 * register this is written in and why the mechanism is stated once here
 * rather than repeated on every card page.
 */
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={SITE_TAGLINE}>
    <article>
        <h1>About {SITE_NAME}</h1>

        <p>
            {SITE_NAME} teaches tarot as structure rather than as a list to
            memorize. Every card is built from the same small set of parts — its
            suit, its number, and the symbols drawn in its artwork — and those
            parts combine the same way from card to card. Learn how suits,
            numbers, and symbols compose, and you can work out what a card is
            doing rather than recalling a caption for it. Forty shared pieces,
            combined, cover all seventy-eight cards.
        </p>

        <p>
            Here is the whole mechanism, stated plainly and only once: a card's
            structure was composed on purpose, so reading one is closer to
            reading a deliberately built visual argument than to predicting what
            will happen. The cards are a prompt for reflection — a way of
            turning a specific question over in a structured way — not a source
            of forecasts. What a reader does with that prompt is theirs to
            decide.
        </p>

        <h2>The deck</h2>
        <p>
            The card images throughout the site come from the classic 1909 deck
            illustrated by Pamela Colman Smith. That deck is in the public
            domain, and every card shown here is scanned from a single coherent
            source, so the artwork stays consistent from card to card rather
            than mixing printings.
        </p>

        <h2>Where things stand</h2>
        <p>
            Most of the written material on this site — card readings, suit and
            number essays, spread instructions — is still a draft. Draft pieces
            are marked as such where they appear, rather than left for a reader
            to discover on their own. Expect the depth and polish of individual
            pages to vary while that work continues.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site won't do</h2>
        <p>A few things are commitments here, not just missing features:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                No streaks, counters, or "you haven't read today's card yet" —
                the practice is offered, not owed, and skipping a day costs
                nothing.
            </li>
            <li>
                No prediction. Readings describe a common way of thinking about
                a card, not what is going to happen.
            </li>
            <li>
                No fixed reversed meanings for individual cards. Reversal is
                taught as a general technique you can apply on top of a card's
                upright meaning, not baked into each card as a second answer.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>
